2. Core Animation Concepts with Real-Life Examples
2.1 Keyframes
Real-life analogy: Like frames in a flipbook. Each keyframe stores a "snapshot" of a bone's position and rotation at a specific time.
// From AnimSequenceFrames.h (uncompressed)
struct KeyFrame {
    CQuaternion Rot;      // Rotation of the bone
    float       DeltaTime; // Time since previous keyframe
};
struct KeyFrameTrans : KeyFrame {
    CVector Trans;         // Position offset (only root bone usually has this)
};
Example: An arm wave animation might have:
- Keyframe 0: Arm at side (t=0.0s)
- Keyframe 1: Arm at 45° (t=0.5s)  
- Keyframe 2: Arm at 90° (t=1.0s)
- Keyframe 3: Arm at 45° (t=1.5s)
- Keyframe 4: Arm at side (t=2.0s)
2.2 Interpolation (LERP & SLERP)
Real-life analogy: The "tweening" between keyframes - like how traditional animators drew "in-between" frames.
// From AnimBlendNode.h:192-195 - Actual interpolation code
if (m_Seq->m_bHasTranslation) {
    trans = lerp<CVector>(kfB->Trans, kfA->Trans, t) * blend;  // Linear interpolation for position
}
if (m_Seq->m_bHasRotation) {
    rot.Slerp(kfB->Rot, kfA->Rot, m_Theta, m_InvSinTheta, t); // Spherical interpolation for rotation
    rot *= blend;
}
- LERP (Linear Interpolation): Used for positions. Moves in a straight line from A to B.
- SLERP (Spherical Linear Interpolation): Used for rotations. Rotates along the shortest arc on a sphere - prevents "gimbal lock" and weird rotation artifacts.
2.3 Blending
Real-life analogy: Like a DJ crossfader mixing two songs. m_BlendAmount is how loud each "song" (animation) is.
From AnimBlendAssociation.h:133-134:
float m_BlendAmount;   // Current blend weight (0.0 = invisible, 1.0 = fully visible)
float m_BlendDelta;    // How fast blend changes per second
How blending works in practice:
Walking (blend 0.6)  +  Running (blend 0.4)  =  Jogging pose
     ↓                      ↓
 60% of walk pose    40% of run pose
From RpAnimBlend.cpp:1187-1188 - the actual blending math:
// Sum rotations (weighted quaternion addition)
nextQ = nextQ + (q.Dot(nextQ) >= 0.f ? q : -q);  // Add quaternions, flip if needed
2.4 Partial Animations
Real-life analogy: Like a green screen effect - you can overlay one animation on specific body parts while another plays on the rest.
Example: CJ can smoke a cigarette (ANIMATION_IS_PARTIAL) while walking. The smoking animation only affects the arm/hand bones, while walking affects legs/torso.
From RpAnimBlend.cpp:616-631:
float CalculateTotalBlendOfPartial(AnimBlendUpdateData* c, AnimBlendFrameData* fd, bool checkDontAddToBlend) {
    float sum{};
    if (c->IncludePartial) {
        for (auto it = c->BlendNodeArrays; *it; it++) {
            const auto node = *it;
            const auto assoc = node->GetAnimAssoc();
            if (assoc->HasFlag(ANIMATION_IS_PARTIAL) && ...) {
                sum += assoc->GetBlendAmount();  // Calculate how much partial anims contribute
            }
        }
    }
    return sum;
}
2.5 Velocity Extraction
Real-life analogy: Instead of the root bone moving the mesh, the movement is "extracted" and applied to the character's world position.
Why? When you animate a walk cycle in Maya/3DS Max, the character moves forward. GTA extracts this movement and applies it to CPed::m_pPosition instead of the skeleton - this way the game controls actual movement speed.
From RpAnimBlend.cpp:1020-1029:
if (!fd->KeyFramesIgnoreNodeTranslation) {
    // Update world positions (This moves the ped around the world)
    const auto wsPos = gpAnimBlendClump->m_PedPosition;
    *wsPos += deltaV - currV;  // Apply velocity delta to world position
    if (hasLoopedVelocity) {
        *wsPos += loopedDeltaV;
    }
    // Update key-frame translation (bone stays in place relative to ped)
    fd->KeyFrame->t = nextT - deltaV + fd->BonePos;
}
